Best Ski Boot Bags of 2026

ski boot bags and ski boot backpacks for traveling with ski or snowboarding boots

October 12th, 2026Home > Gear Reviews > Ski & Snow

Ski trips often begin and end with the boot shuffle: carrying heavy, awkward footwear through airports, shuttles, parking lots, and snowy paths. A good ski boot bag makes that process easier by protecting your boots, organizing the rest of your gear, and giving you a more practical way to carry everything to and from the mountain.

We researched dozens of ski boot bags and ski boot backpacks, comparing specs, design features, media reviews, and user feedback. Then we tested the most promising options ourselves for more than a year on ski road trips, backcountry outings, and international travel, including a ski trip to Japan.

We evaluated each bag for protection, organization, durability, carry comfort, capacity, and weather resistance. We also paid attention to padding around boots, helmets, and goggles; boot-specific compartments; straps and carry systems; and how well each bag handled snow, slush, and regular travel use.

Top Pick: The Thule RoundTrip Boot Pack 60L is our Best Overall ski boot bag. It stood out for its protective padding, thoughtful organization, durable materials, and comfortable backpack-style design. It has enough room for boots and additional ski gear while still being manageable for travel, making it the bag we’d reach for first on most ski trips.

Carrying your skis while you travel? See our guide to Best Ski Travel Bag and Best Snowboard Travel Bag

We create reader-supported, objective gear reviews independently selected by our editors. This story may contain affiliate links, which help fund our website. When you click on the links to purchase gear, we may get a commission – without costing you an extra cent. Thank you for supporting our work and mission of outdoor coverage for every body! Learn more.

Ski boot bags comparison table

*See our Deals Page for discounts and sales on products that win our awards.

The winners

Best overall Ski boot bag: Thule RoundTrip Boot Pack 60L

Material: 600D polyester, bluesign-approvedWeight: 2 lb 12 ozVolume: 45L or 60LPockets: one boot garage, one main compartment, one goggle pocketPadding: thin padding on sides, additional padding on top and inside boot garageWhat we liked: protective, durable, spacious, and very functionalWhat we didn’t like: expensive and a little cumbersome

If you’re looking for a snowboard or ski boot bag with all the bells and whistles (except the unnecessary ones), the Thule RoundTrip boot backpack takes the cake for our best overall boot bag. A few key features level this bag up from others we researched and tested. The main features we appreciate are the padding and durability. This bag has light padding throughout the sides but boasts extra rigidity on the top, which protects ski helmets and ski goggles when in transit. This bag made us feel confident that our gear was safe inside, even if it got tossed around a little.

This 60-liter ski and snowboard boot bag is built like a backpack and has a rear boot compartment that’s zippered and accessed from the back panel. Inside, two sides are lined with a durable, padded, and water-resistant layer that keeps your boots safe and sound inside. In addition, the polyester material is resistant to snags and tears. With solid stitching and a quality design, this ski boot backpack will stand the test of time.

The Thule Ski Boot Bag has great stitching and well-placed handles as well as buckles to cinch down extra volume (left). Close-up of how ski boots fit within the main compartment in the Thule RoundTrip boot bag (top). The Thule RoundTrip ski boot bag has a spacious main compartment that can be accessed from the top and a front zipper, so it’s easy to get in and out (bottom).

Compartments

The bag's spacious main compartment can be accessed from a top zipper and a front zipper, making it easy to get gear in and out.

A separate, smaller, and well-padded pocket at the top holds ski goggles, while a helmet nests nicely at the top of the main compartment.

Capacity

On a ski road trip to Utah, we could fit boots, a ski helmet, goggles, ski gloves, a ski jacket and ski pants, and some extra layers and essentials like ski socks in this one pack. And during travel, we never worried about dings in the helmet or goggles inside, thanks to that extra padding and rigidity on top. This bag is just small enough to pass as a carry-on, depending on what airline you are flying and how vigilant the flight attendants are. We did not have a chance to fly with it, but it would be our choice if we did because of the extra protection.

Value

The only downside to this bag is the high price tag. It costs about $74 more than my budget winner. However, if you plan to travel often with your ski equipment and want a durable, protective, spacious bag that will last a long time, we think it’s worth the splurge.

The RoundTrip boot bag is a bestseller on REI, with almost all 5-star reviews from everyday customers. You’d be hard-pressed to find a more functional boot bag than this one.

